// `#run` evaluates an arbitrary expression at compile time in the bytecode
// VM. The result is interned as a compile-time value, indistinguishable
// from a literal.
COMPUTED :: #run add(2, 3);

add :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> s64 {
    return a + b;
}

// `#run` may also appear as a top-level statement, and is evaluated during compilation.
//
// **It has no side effect anything can observe, and this comment used to claim it did.** Compile-time
// code may call no `#foreign` procedure (`crates/jr-vm/src/interp.rs:1368`, ADR-0006) and may not read a
// file-scope global (ADR-0186 §2) — so it cannot print, write a file, or even allocate, since
// `Basic.malloc` is `#foreign`. What a bare `#run` does is *run*: its own frame, its own arithmetic, and
// whatever it returns, which for a statement is discarded.
//
// The distinction matters because `docs/build-script-plan.md` rests on it: a build script written as a
// `#run` could do nothing at all, which is why that plan runs one as an ordinary program instead.
#run report();

report :: () {
}

main :: () {
    // Constant-folded before codegen ever sees it.
    total := COMPUTED + 1;
}
